From 763dca17bd48e828b897e3989a298d87cee46e53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 00:40:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] odb: replace_hier_module copies child module instances
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

dbModInst::swapMaster copied only the leaf instances of the module it
swapped in (_dbModule::copy: "TODO: handle hierarchical child
instances") and named every new net after the base name of the old
one. A module with a child instance aborted: the child's net
inner/_013_ and the module's own _013_ both became <inst>/_013_, which
trips the assert in copyModuleInsts in a debug build and, in an
optimized one, makes dbNet::create return null and the swap stop with
"Attempt to connect iterm ... to a null net" (ODB-0440). The copy of
the swapped-out module that swapMaster saves to a child block had the
same naming, and there two nets of one name were silently merged into
one: the module's internal net _003_ and the net _003_ of its parent
that reaches it through a port (seen in replace_hier_mod2 and
replace_hier_mod6, whose LCU/ALU modules have such nets).

Now:

- _dbModule::copy recreates each child dbModInst of the module, with a
  uniquified copy of its dbModule, ports and mod iterms, recursively
  (copyModuleChildren), copies the leaf instances of every module into
  the copy of their module, and connects the children's mod iterms to
  the copied mod nets.
- Nets keep their names relative to the root of the copied hierarchy,
  as link_design -hier names them: inner/_013_ under instance u becomes
  u/inner/_013_. A net named outside the old hierarchy, one that
  crosses its boundary, is named after the port's mod net. New nets are
  mapped from the old dbNet, not by name, and a name already taken
  stops with ODB-0481 instead of the assert.
- A net is internal when all its iterms are in the copied hierarchy,
  not only in its root module, and the boundary check walks up to the
  root module's mod net.
- swapMaster connects a net that crosses the boundary to every iterm
  the port reaches through the mod nets of the child modules, not only
  to the root module's iterms; it saves the old module to its child
  block before deleting its mod inst, so the saved nets keep their
  relative names, and it deletes the old module's child modules with
  it instead of leaving them behind uninstantiated.

replace_hier_mod7 swaps Outer (one child) for Outer_new (two levels of
children, copied from an instance in the top block), back to Outer
(copied from the child block the first swap saved it to), undoes a
swap in an ECO and swaps again; children and parents share the names
_1_ and _013_. It stops with ODB-0440 before this change. After the
swap the worst path through r2/D, its slack (0.8075 ns) and the net
names are those of the design linked from Verilog with Outer_new in
place. replace_hier_mod1-6, replace_hier_mod_report_checks,
TestSwapMaster*, rsz's replace_arith_modules1-3 pass unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
